#year1commentary	
coal0101	Tim: Can you imagine actually pulling yourself up a little chain of ties that are as long as the, um... Like--
coal0102	Peter M: Well, that's what's great about-- And then I like the view down.
coal0103	Tim: He's so calm about it, too. He's like, "Hyup hyup hyup hyup, ah! Many stories." I mean part of it is, I guess, he probably won't die.
coal0104	Peter M: He's already dead, I was going to say. He's already dead. So it's really just--
coal0105	Tim: And I was thinking, does he have the muscles to do that? Or else... No, he doesn't have any muscles. He's just a living skeleton. How is he moving? He's magic.
coal0106	Peter M: His spirit is animating, yes.
coco0201	Tim: The computers in the game are, um, I think where you can see Peter Chan's "Twelve Monkeys"-like influence. Like Terry Gilliam mix of, like... I don't know if you call it steampunk, but there's a lot of analog big tube-type things combined with Art Deco and old-fashioned typewriter keyboards.
coco0202	Peter M: Well, they had "steam" and "punk" in those days, right? But they didn't actually call it steampunk, right?
coco0203	Tim: The nineties? I think we might have had steampunk back then. Oh yeah.
coco0204	Peter M: Jeez. See, I told you I was from the "Mad Men" era.
coco0205	Tim: I'm so glad they made a show about you guys.
cofe0101	Tim: I don't know why we chose Robert Frost for the balloon shape, either.
cofe0102	Peter M: Oh, that was genius--
cofe0103	Tim: I just knew that it would be hard to make Robert Frost.
cofe0104	Peter M: Yes! And also because-- I think the whole implication is that the clown is way, way beyond his pay grade, and he has disdain for all those around him. And so he makes shapes that his lowly audience--
cofe0105	Tim: I never even thought about that being a testimony to the skills of the clown. I thought of it as Manny being a smartass. 'cause he asked for it. He's like, "Oh yeah? Can you do Robert Frost?" And then, of course, the clown does it.
cofe0106	Peter M: But, see, that's a Chandler thing, too, because the detective is always smarter than sort of the world that he's in. So he's teasing the clown.
cofe0107	Tim: So to speak.
cofe0108	Peter M: Yeah, so to speak. 'cause he knows the clown knows what Robert Frost looks like.
coga0101	Julian: You hear reverb on the steps, and you hear the reverb on his voice. And that was real-time reverb in a time when we never did that. We always prebaked the files with reverb. Jeff really had to fight for, I think it was, like, five percent of the CPU. But now anything that happens in this garage goes through that reverb. And, like, to modern game folks--audio people--it's just totally basic. No big deal. But back then it was such a big deal! And he really had to jump through hoops for it, and it's so great.
coga0201	Tim: Mark Hamer-- I remember [him] animating Glottis coming out. And he had to put this thing called a lattice on his butt. And so, "What are you working on, Mark?" "Oh, I'm working on the Glottis butt lattice." And he was always working on the Glottis butt lattice to morph and squash and stretch his butt as it came out of the door. And so, three cheers to Mark Hamer and his Glottis butt lattice. Amazing work still remembered to this day.
coha0101	Bret: So this was the first scene in the game that we ever actually did with some test assets. So I had to-- This was when I was proving out, "Okay, yes, we can do this." It was this hallway scene. We had Manny running up and down. We had the security-cam kind of camera switching. We had text on the screen. We had animations. We had geolocation of the sound relative to the camera--the typing was down the hallway. And we had Manny running up and down. It was a really good test scene and it kind of came through at the very last second. Literally, I was compiling code as people ran in the room because I had an awful bug. Um, but this was sort of like proof that, yes, we have all the elements to make this work. This was the first scene ever put together. And notice in the scene Manny hardly ever clips behind anything, and that's partially because I didn't have z-buffers working yet. But it was good enough that when people saw it they were really impressed, and I was kind of surprised because I was like the new guy at LucasArts and I was kind of half-assing my way through this and somehow made everybody really impressed. At that point I thought, "Okay, we can do this."
coha0201	Tim: My writing has changed a lot since this. Like, I was really trying to write good dialogue here, which to me at the time meant a lot of puns and like, um, word play. Like, "This suit is-- This deck of cards is a lot like me: it's ragged around the edges but I've got more suits..." I forget what it is, but everything had to be this kind of, like, bon mot.
coha0202	Peter M: Well, that's kind of Chandleresque, too. I mean, you know...
coha0203	Tim: Kind of, but I think I ease off on it in act two. I think I just start writing-- Or, in year two, there's a lot more just normal talking. Which I think I kind of like a little bit more now. I mean, it's fun to have crazy word play like that but you can just kind of trip over yourself a lot--too much--if you focus on that, I think.
coha0301	Peter M: So is there a reason that a lot of people who are in administrative positions of power have New York accents? Is that a...
coha0302	Tim: Everyone in the, um, jazz era was from New York.
coha0303	Peter M: Well, yeah, that's true.
coha0304	Tim: A lot of people don't know that.
cole0101	Bret: The ties. When Manny later uses the ties to cross over to the roof. [Tim] wanted it to be more like a little, you know, a little bit less adventure game and more sort of skill game, where you had to kind of, you know, line up the ties and throw the lasso and hook it on the thing. And I'll be honest with you, that was technically not something I was prepared to do in any way, shape, or form. It was kind of all I could do to make the rest of the stuff work. So, um, the ties and the beavers were--and actually the shocks on the tree--were all supposed to be a little bit more dynamic than a lot of the canned stuff you typically get in adventure games. We just couldn't do it.
cole0201	Peter M: I always liked the music up on the ledge.
cole0202	Tim: Me too! It was very Bernard Herrmann--"Vertigo."
cole0203	Peter M: And it always made me feel like a little... Maybe it's 'cause I actually have a little bit of vertigo, or whatever you say.
cole0204	Tim: But isn't that-- That happens a lot in "Vertigo"--the movie "Vertigo." Like the, um...
cole0205	Peter M: Yep, yep. Yeah, it probably comes from that.
cole0206	Tim: That dreamy, falling sensation.
cole0207	Peter M: Well, I'm a huge Bernard Herrmann fan. How can you not be? He was the guy who really brought kind of a seriousness of classical music to scoring. I mean, the guys before... There were great composers before him, they were a little more sort of, kind of the entertaining aspect of orchestral music. I think he was more really very serious about it.
colo0101	Bret: Ah, elevator code. Elevator code is the worst, man. Door code in general.
colo0102	Kevin: So there was a door box that you would draw on the ground. That would have data attached to it that would trigger the art and the, uh...
colo0103	Bret: Well, it's like the old-- Like when you walk into a supermarket in the old days, they didn't have a IR thing that was pointing at you. They had like a pad on the ground. You would roll your cart or step on the pad on the ground and it would automatically open the grocery store for you. It's just like that. There's a little invisible box that, uh, you cross that triggers all that stuff.
colo0104	Kevin: And then all the fiddly bits of people step on the box, step off the box, step back on it, while the doors are opening and closing. Those doors were broken for a long time.
colo0201	Peter T: Savvy viewers will note that the lobby is based on a real building in San Francisco with Mexican architecture. Uh, or Mayan Deco architecture. 450 Sutter, yeah, which is an actual building... Which is the inspiration for the whole business. Yeah, San Francisco is kind of a-- There's a lot of Deco-ish stuff around town, and that was a big part of making this world look the way it looked.
como0201	Gaurav: So this was actually the art test I took. I had to make this whole scene in a day.
como0202	Dave: Really?
como0203	Mark: Did they pay you for that?
como0204	Dave: One day?
como0205	Gaurav: Barely. I was so scared.
como0206	Dave: How much did you get done?
como0207	Gaurav: Uh, I think almost all the geometry. In some cases it used to be an overnight render.
como0301	Tim: I love pneumatic tubes! If you ever read articles about old New York, there's a lot of stuff about how the tube system was... There was an interbuilding tube system, too.
como0302	Peter M: Oh, really?
como0303	Tim: I don't know how that could possibly work! And the tubes were sixteen inches in diameter, and they would shoot pneumatic messages, like, they'd push them between buildings. Now those tubes are there. They're thinking about using them for fiber-optics and stuff like that.
coos0101	Tim: I love that mural 'cause it's the three ways of travelling in the Land of the Dead: the car, the train, and the boat. There's no air travel in the Land of the Dead. No "Land of the Dead Airlines."
copk0101	Alex: Down in QA, we got on the project way, way earlier than we ever had on any other project and we would play the game with the concept art on the screen. But Manny was modeled, and done, and lit... Well, maybe he wasn't lit, I don't know, but he would zoom around the concept art, and it was all black and white. And uh, that's why I was...
copk0102	Paul: Oh, that would be neat!
copk0103	Alex: Yeah.
cost0101	Dave: I think the camera choices in this game were actually pretty fantastic as well. I don't know who was responsible for that. Was it storyboarded or...
cost0102	Mark: Was that you, Pete?
cost0103	Peter T: Yeah, probably. A little bit. A lot of stuff got worked out ahead of time, certainly. I mean because, you know, we didn't necessarily build full three-sixty 3-D worlds. I mean except for like the offices and stuff.
cotu0101	Dave: You know, the games we make nowadays we try to keep them real-time. And the environments will come in the tens of thousands of polygons, probably. But, uh, or at least under a hundred, I would say at least at the TellTale studio. But here they I think were almost unlimited, unless it crashed the machines all the time.
cotu0102	Peter T: Yes, it was unlimited, and it was like trying to render it on your iPhone, which was crazy.
cotu0103	Mark: He modeled like every single nut and bolt everywhere in there, and they were all high-res.
cotu0104	Gaurav: Yeah, I was about to say a certain amount of this was us not--
cotu0105	Dave: "It might get a close-up!"
cotu0106	Gaurav: ... really knowing and us not being efficient about how we set everything up.
cotu0107	Mark: As an animator, when you open one of these environments to animate in, you had to basically throw everything away except for the one thing that Manny touches or walks next to or something.
cobv0101	Bret: This scene, actually, this is, uh-- The beavers here are another good example of like, you know, why you have to have coroutines in games because, you--at least, in adventure games. You have to have like a script that just says, "How's this beaver going to behave? What's he going to do?" And at the time that people were making games, scripting engines weren't popular yet, you had a lot of games in C [programming language]. And somebody would've been trying to write in their game engine, in their game loop, "And go run the beavers now." In a scene like this you had to be able to take that and say, "This is how a beaver behaves. Right now I'm thinking like a beaver. How would a beaver-- He's going to crawl over here, he's going to chew on these things. Then he'll get tired, then he'll jump over here." Same thing with the pigeons and everything else. And that, again, I didn't have to think about the fact that they were needed. They were obviously needed. I mean, that's one of the things that SCUMM had made very obvious, like, you cannot make adventure games without coroutines. They didn't even call them coroutines, though. I mean, we didn't even know what to call them for a long time until--
cobv0102	Kevin: Threads.
cobv0103	Bret: They were just threads, yeah. And actually, well that was one of the early experiments. I actually tried to use actual Windows threads. 'cause this was a Windows game; it wasn't a DOS game. This was a Windows game. We can use actual threads! I initially did try and actually have different Lua threads, and it was like, it was just so heavyweight. Machines are so tight and it was so much overhead. And it was so complex. It was like, we need something lightweight. It needs to be really focused on what the game does.
cobv0201	Lleslle: This one was, um--
cobv0202	Julian: How could you ever cut this?
cobv0203	Lleslle: I know, but programmatically it was buggy. And it caused-- It was hard. No one understood how to solve this puzzle for quite a long time. And so I was like, "Look, you gotta pick something," and we hadn't started it yet. So this is a classic Tim technique. When you want to cut something, he will start putting it in the game. So that everything gets in. So, uh, yeah. Funny, funny.
cobv0301	Tim: They were-- We were like, "Where is the fire-- What is the flame source for the beavers?" And they were like, "Well, they have a nuclear core." They have a nuclear core. So they're thermonuclear flaming bone beavers.
cobv0302	Peter M: Yeah, you know, I have to say. Flaming bone beavers. I mean--
cobv0303	Tim: You would think that would be controversial. A band? A band name.
cobv0304	Peter M: Maybe, yeah.
cobd0101	Tim: I still remember writing a big justification about tank controls when I was pitching this. People were like, "Let's make it so the controls are more like Mario." Where you push-- No, no, point-and-click. I was thinking-- Tank controls I thought were superior to point-and-click at the time because when you're pointing and clicking you're distant from the screen, and the screen is this other thing that you're interacting with at your finger tips.
cobd0102	Peter M: Right, you're seeing--
cobd0103	Tim: But when you're doing tank controls, you feel like you are in the center of the character. The world is rotating around you. So when you think-- Instead of going, "I want to go on the left of the screen" or "I want to go on the right of the screen," you're saying, "I want to look at the thing to the left of me, and the thing to the right of me." And now I don't know if that was necessarily the right decision, but it's a good piece of evidence that you can somehow, sometimes make a really good argument for the wrong decision, and that's something to watch out for in life. You know what I mean? You can make a really compelling [case], and in fact win an argument, and be totally wrong.
cobd0104	Peter M: Winning an argument has nothing to do with being right, that's for sure.
cobd0105	Tim: Exactly, exactly. It's just being able to make a good case.
cotu0201	Tim: This line that he says a lot actually comes from the old IT guy at LucasArts. We'd say, "Hey, the server's down." And he goes, "The server's not down. It's never down. You just can't get to it right now."
cona0101	Lleslle: Well, this is the area that Tim and I would make a lot of jokes about because, you know. Producer-type person, I need to shorten the game. Cut it somehow. So this area, I would say, "Hey, we need to cut... the beavers. Does that make sense?"
cona0102	Julian: He's like, "No way, not cutting it."
cona0103	Lleslle: Well, he-- The whole time we'd fight about it, and it was that one kind of thing where, um... That was the story I told a little earlier, about the ship, you know? He said, "Lleslle, a game is like a ship, and the ship is going, and it's going to keep going until it stops." And I was like, what kind of logic is that? It has to finish at some point. So I was like, "Look, the beavers gotta go." They'd always be on the list. And he actually drew me little pictures on a napkin of, like, the armadillo, the beavers on fire. And he's like, "Why did you cut me, Lleslle? Please don't cut me, Lleslle." And he would just poke me.
cona0104	Julian: That's so funny.
cona0105	Lleslle: Yeah.
colb0101	Tim: This is like a callback to, um, "Those Brave Young Men and Their Jaunty Jalopies." You ever see that movie? "Those Outrageous Young Men and Their Flying Machines."
colb0102	Peter M: Oh, that I remember.
colb0103	Tim: They're these two crazy movies from the sixties, which were all about-- It was kind of like the "Wacky Races," but a real life version--
colb0104	Peter M: [singing] "Those magnificent men and their flying machines, ey!"
colb0105	Tim: It was kind of like "Wild, Wild, Wild World," or whatever--
colb0106	Peter M: "It's a Mad, Mad, Mad, Mad World."
colb0107	Tim: Where all these people are chasing some crazy prize. But the cars in it always had things like that. I think that is a direct lift. Like, one of the cars would have these hydraulic shocks that lifted up, or just crazy extending things.
colr0101	Tim: What is this crazy music? How did you get it all warp-sounding?
colr0102	Peter M: Well, that's a great... See, remember that when I did all the themes for "Grim" I carried around a little handheld cassette, right? So I'd go for my hike in the hills in the morning and I'd carry my little cassette with me and if something popped in my head I would hum into it.
colr0103	Tim: And it dropped into a puddle?
colr0104	Peter M: Well, those cassettes... I don't know if you remember much about cassette technology, 'cause, you know--
colr0105	Tim: I remember cassettes. I made some mixtapes.
colr0106	Peter M: They didn't always last--especially the little, tiny ones--didn't last for a super long time. So if you used it all the time, which I did to compose, the machine heads--the mechanism--would start to wear out. Especially if it ran low on batteries. So yeah, that was me sitting at the piano, working on that theme for the Land of the Living which I recorded on a little handheld cassette, and then played it back with batteries that were running out. And recorded that into the digital system.
colr0107	Tim: That's awesome. Was that an accident at first? It ran out of power at an earlier time, writing something else?
colr0108	Peter M: You know, I can't remember. I think it was an accident, but I kind of meant to do it, too.
colr0201	Tim: In the Land of the Living, the people look horrifying because of that-- Have you ever seen that optical illusion where you take a picture of someone from a magazine, you cut rectangles out of their eyes and their mouth, and then turn them upside down? And they just look like demons.
colr0202	Peter M: Oh, yeah, yeah. Totally.
colr0203	Tim: That's the Land of the Living.
codo0101	Bret: There's a scene here. So there's the wine bottle there on the side. Or sorry, the bourbon bottle. And there's the taking a slug of his booze. That was actually-- We had lots of math problems early in the game where we were lining up camera angles, like you mentioned. These camera angles, if we had it off between the real-time and the pre-rendered stuff, things got kind of messy. That was one of the first scenes we had where Manny as one character goes and interacts with another item in the scene, and it also has to be placed very precisely in the scene because it has to appear to be on the shelf. And it was really difficult to coordinate, you know. He has to walk to exactly the right spot, line up to perform his animation. The cup has its own animation of flying through the air to his mouth. There's all these weird ways we handled it. I think we changed that later to handle it better, so that the cup was actually attached, we could swap it in. Blank out the one that was on the shelf, put in the one that was in his hand. It's like a magic trick. Originally, that was something we used to test it out and make it work. It was-- I remember spending days on that. Just being worried like, "Oh man, we're never going to get this to align perfectly between 2-D and 3-D."
codo0201	Peter T: I remember something about the mural. This would be the mural in, uh... What's that guy's name? The nemesis's office.
codo0202	Dave: Domino?
codo0203	Peter T: Domino, yeah. The mural on the ceiling of Domino's office. There was something. I think maybe that angle wasn't planned or something in the game, and then whoever did the textures for that room put this crazy mural on there. And so it was like, "We have to show the mural! We have to get that up there." I remember there was some sort of fiasco around that mural.
codo0204	Dave: Usually, it's the other way around. Usually artists put a bunch of beautiful work into something and then someone turns the camera the other way and doesn't show it.
codo0205	Paul: Right, exactly.
codo0301	Tim: How is he sweating without skin? So inaccurate. Tried to get it really accurate with the punching. You know: left, left, right, right, left, left, right... I think that's how you do it. Well, you don't just do one, two, one, two with the speed bag.
codo0302	Peter M: Yeah, I think you're right. Yeah it looks--
codo0303	Tim: In my boxing history.
codo0304	Peter M: My grandpa used to have a boxing bag in his basement. He showed me how it works once.
cosm0101	Darragh: That line right there, "Love is for the living. I'm only after her for one reason. She's my ticket out of here," that was the key line that Tony and I would always go back to, that kind of identified who that character was. The idea with Manny being a Hispanic Humphrey Bogart-- That line evoked "Casablanca" in many ways and, um, that was kind of just... We worked on that line longer than we should have. But it was just one of those that we just felt was important to kind of nail and help define Manny.
cosm0102	Clint: And you could see Pete's musical treatment of it. He kind of suspended the music, and had this little classical guitar melody that had a feeling of being like a pocket in time, to further highlight that line, yeah.
cogs0101	Tim: I never realized how weird it was that he gets locked into a room by his boss, which I don't think is legal.
cogs0102	Peter M: Oh, you know back in the thirties you could do all kinds of stuff. You know, rubber hoses for cops and so on. It was just fifty years after the Old West.
cogs0103	Tim: Whoa, that's weird to think about.
cogs0104	Peter M: Yeah.
cohq0101	Peter T: There's some concept art there in the PowerPoint.
cohq0102	Paul: Yeah, 'cause I remember I just took [Peter Chan's] stuff and, like, inverted it to make it look like a plan.
cohq0103	Peter T: Oh, really? You took someone else's textures and reused them.
cohq0104	Paul: Great artists borrow... No, wait. Good artists steal. Um, yeah.
cohq0201	Tim: Oh, the secretary has a dual identity! It's always lots of fun with dual identities. I like that Eva gets to have a little, like, um... She's got more going on that you thought she did.
cohq0202	Peter M: I love that, too. And I like that there's something fishy in the Department of Death, too. That's a good twist.
como0101	Darragh: This is one of the scenes where we had Tony and Maria Canals in working together. They're both from Cuba and had worked together before they were cast in Grim. So it was kind of fun to get the two of them together again and play off of each other, which is of course the way you'd like to do any game.
corf0101	Tim: I used to work at a doctor's office, and one of the jobs-- Just terrible jobs there. And one of them was the roof was covered in, like, six inches of pigeon guano up there. They had a flat roof like the roof of this building. And they're like, "Look, we gotta get that stuff off the roof. It's really, it's going to break the roof. There's so much pigeon crap on the roof." And I had to go up there with this flat shovel. It was kind of like cutting brownies out of a pan. I was just chopping up the bird poop and just scooping it up and throwing it off the roof. But in chopping it up I would occasionally find, like in sedimentary rock, like an archaeologist or a paleontologist, I would find the bones of a dead pigeon just inside this weird brownie of guano on the roof.
corf0102	Peter M: Wow.
corf0103	Tim: So I had my fair share of bad jobs.
corf0104	Peter M: This really, this was really a kind of therapy to get that traumatic experience--
corf0105	Tim: Yeah, there's not enough poop on the roof to make this really--
corf0106	Peter M: Well, it's hard to indicate that with the technology of the time. Kind of just like-- It's like if you did wind in a 8-bit game it just sounded like [8-bit wind noise]. It just sounded bad, you know.
corf0107	Tim: At least I got to clear the pigeons from the roof of my memories.
core0101	Tim: I really wanted to have this moment of Velasco bonding with Glottis through the magic of cars, talking about engines and stuff...
core0102	Tim: Cars bring people together. Talking about engines.
core0103	Tim: Because I never had sports--
core0104	Peter M: Internal combustion...
core0105	Tim: I never liked sports. I was not into the manly unifier of sports, but--
core0106	Peter M: Me neither.
core0107	Tim: Cars you can talk about with the toughest of dudes.
core0108	Peter M: That's true, that's true.
cori0101	Darragh: The character Celso is played by a really funny actor called Peter Lurie. When he came into the studio, the first day, he had this real look of apprehension about him. It turned out the word in Hollywood was that "Grim Fandango" was code name for "Star Wars: Episode I" casting. So when he was coming in for the project people were telling him, "You're going to be going in, and George Lucas is going to be there. They're going to be flying you up to the ranch!" He was terribly disappointed when he walked in and the only person in the studio was me.
cori0201	Peter M: When you run into Celso Flores again and there's that-- You know, he's always down and out and he's listening to this music on the radio while he's working, which is actually Hector's music. All of the-- Most of the jazz pieces are some variation on Hector's theme, and they're being piped in everywhere. Like every time you listen to something on the radio it's actually Hector's music and it's sort of this idea that Hector is behind everything and in control of all the-- Pulling all the strings of commerce, and then here's this little guy. Celso's mopping the floor and with this reminder that he's just a little guy in a big world with these big forces.
cori0202	Tim: That's awesome.
cori0203	Peter M: Does that sound too philosophical?
cori0204	Tim: I think that works on a primal level. I don't think I was conscious of that, but I think it was working.
cori0301	Tim: I love the tiling floor. It's my favorite part in this one. And the fact that it's a automat, which was part of a period reference, too. Like those ways that you-- There used to be these restaurants in New York where you'd go--or big cities--where you'd go in and the food was ready for you behind these little panels, and you would just open the door and take it. Which is an interesting way of serving stuff. Like just separate the server from the eater, I guess? I don't know.
cori0302	Peter M: Oh, that is, I never--
cori0303	Tim: Like you don't want to see the wait staff, so they're behind this wall, because of some weird social reason.
cori0304	Peter M: That is weird.
cori0305	Tim: And they didn't really have automats anymore, but I always thought that's what was behind that.
cori0306	Peter M: It does seem a little impersonal, doesn't it?
cosp0101	Tim: This is me playing with my own worst fears. I was realizing while we were making this game, randomly I was thinking about how nice it is that spiders can't fly. You know, with spiders-- Spiders are kind of icky, but they're on the wall. You can stay away from them. Don't go in the garage. Don't get fire wood, ever. You can stay away from spiders. But what if they could fly? What if you had instead of a bee buzzing around you or something, you had to worry about like, there's a black widow. Wouldn't that be creepy? So we made flying spiders in the game.
cosg0101	Clint: We coined a phrase called "McConnellian" ambience, meaning Pete McConnell. His style of ambience where you have a little idea, and then a completely different idea, and then a completely different idea, just sort of sequenced. The texture would get kind of big, and then it would get very small with a solo clarinet. Then something completely different would happen and then you'd get a little bassoon. And so these ideas would waft in and out like clouds, or just passing notions. It's a great way to keep the listener entertained but not get in his way. So we termed that a McConnellian approach to ambience.
cosg0201	Tim: I always like Glottis because he's just so pure in his-- I mean, he's by definition pure in that he's just the spirit of driving, kind of. Someone from the land itself. He has no-- Given nothing but a desire to drive and go fast, and all his motivations are about like, um, moving at high speed. He almost dies because he's going too slow at some point and he has to go fast again to come back to life. But he also obviously cares about friendship and Manny, and he's a very loyal character.
cotr0101	Clint: I remember in this room--as in several others, Julian--we used a sound ambience that we recorded using a contact microphone on various machines, like Coke machines, other vibrating devices or machine-like devices that produces a kind of interesting hum. If you put your ear up against a Coke machine, or-- I was just on the Golden Gate Bridge last week and I stuck my ear up against one of those three hundred, two hundred foot pylons and it's amazing the resonances that are going on in those type of resonant bodies. And we would record things like that and turn them into ambiences for some of the rooms.
cotr0201	Tim: Some of the things that we came up with for this game, like the, um... petrified forest. Petrified forest was right out of a Bogart movie, right? That comes from the name of a Bogart movie.
cotr0202	Peter M: And he's actually a gangster.
cotr0203	Tim: He's a bad guy in it, yeah. But, um, having these little things pumping-- I don't even know where this, like, came up with... I feel like in games now we try to make things kind of make sense. Like, oh, you go to a gas station, something you know that exists. But here it's a weird hydraulic pump factory with a counterweight on top of it for sucking something out of the marrow out of these trees.
cotr0204	Peter M: Oh, right.
cotr0205	Tim: But it was all worth it 'cause it was satisfying to see Glottis spin around on the top of it.
cotr0206	Peter M: I like the environmental message myself.
#year2commentary	
cocb0101	Lleslle: And just the amount of research that went into all of this, 'cause I just remember Tim going to every film noir movie, ever. And we would just-- All the time, there would be something to do. And he's like, "Wanna go to the movies?" And it was just, I mean you can see it. The dark shadows, and then the music. I mean, everything is just so film noir and really pulls you in.
cocb0102	Darragh: Yeah, and I remember us all going to see "Casablanca." I think it was in The Castro Theatre. It was one of those old San Francisco theaters. I forget which one it was, but it was, a whole gang of us went.
cocb0103	Lleslle: Yeah, remember-- Did you guys go to the, um, Golden Gate Fields for the horse races?
cocb0104	Darragh: I didn't do that.
cocb0105	Clint: I wish.
cocb0106	Lleslle: That was part of it, 'cause I think there were some scenes around that. And that was just fun, I mean, just the research trips.
cocb0107	Darragh: Yeah.
cocb0108	Lleslle: Those were fun.
cotw0101	Tim: You hear the cats racing? We were always so sad that we never showed the cat races, but that was for budget reasons, obviously. We didn't want to cut the actual cats. But I wanted to have giant cats running on a track... Like that!
cotw0102	Peter M: And the idea of cats racing, being herdable enough to race is pretty... Why cats?
cotw0103	Tim: Um, why cats? There was a... God, I don't remember. Why cats? That's a good idea.
cotw0104	Peter M: I mean, I just remember making the music, going [cat yowling noises] at some point, 'cause you're near the kitty litter.
cotw0105	Tim: Mm hm, mm hm.
cotb0101	Bret: You know, there are all these background scripts that do things as you enter a room. It's like it's, you know, "Start all these scripts when you enter the room. Do all these backgrounds actions." Then we leave the room, supposed to stop all those scripts. And it was all left to chance--left to programmer error. And, uh, there was one. We were talking about this earlier. There's a script that runs the cat races, and it runs the announcer for the cat races. And it actually, true to Tim's attention to detail he wanted it to actually simulate cat races, and--
cotb0102	Kevin: There are actual cat races going on.
cotb0103	Bret: There are cats that are being raced and they, the same name will show up and you'll hear this cat getting ahead of the other one. I think Chris Purvis did that.
cotb0104	Kevin: Yeah, and the odds are real. The winnings are real. Just...
cotb0105	Bret: Manny's wallet fills up during the course of the game... No, the cat races are running and so in the background the announcer is running, and it's the simulation of these cat races being run. And, um, yeah they never killed that script. So you play the rest of the game from that cat scene and the cat race simulator script is always running.
cotb0106	Kevin: Yes, from the moment the cat races start to the end credits of the game.
cotb0107	Bret: To the end of time.
cotb0108	Kevin: The cat race simulation is going on in the background.
cotb0109	Bret: There are still kitties racing today.
cotb0110	Kevin: Yes.
cobk0101	Dave: In the final version of the game, are the backgrounds actually just pre-rendered flats?
cobk0102	Peter T: Yes, they are.
cobk0103	Dave: See, that's amazing to me 'cause we live in a world of 3-D now. We're used to floating the camera around inside of these things. But, you know, once you chose your camera angle to pre-render something, that was it. There you go.
cobk0104	Peter T: No, it's Manny walking around in a blank 3-D room with z-depth data to clip him in front of the stuff that he needs to clip in front of.
cobb0101	Tim: I'm a strong believer in things that should be-- When you're making stuff, it should be something that could only have been made by you, or in that group of people and at the time you made it, in the place you made it.
cobb0102	Peter M: Yes.
cobb0103	Tim: You know? I think "Grim" is definitely that case. Like, only-- That's one of the great things about "Grim." I think it's a collection of amazing artists, like you, and the visual artists, and the programmers, and the actors. And everything that came together for that game was just such a-- Was almost overwhelming distraction of amazing artists coming together. Like, "Oh my God, look at that background! Oh my God, listen to this music! Oh my God, listen to that voice!" So I think it makes that game really special. But it also has a lot of personal details that are just kind of woven into it that makes it. So like even if we were making this game from scratch, with the same intentions today, it would just be completely different.
cobp0101	Tim: I don't know why there's so much, uh, union jokery in games that I've worked on. But I've always found it, um, maybe because it was a big part of, like, movies of that era, like "On the Waterfront." You know, and not so much unions but mobster corrupt unions.
cobp0102	Peter M: Right.
cobp0103	Tim: There's always a big part of that, you know, going on. And then also, like, worker/anti-worker Communist themes going on. Like, it was all tied together. And I don't know if that all sprung out of "On the Waterfront", or...
cobp0104	Peter M: Well, "On the Waterfront" though was kind of, um... I mean, it was... sort of championing the... whatever, the plight of the working man, but it also was talking about the dark side of the union thing, for sure.
cobp0105	Tim: But it was a corrupt union, it was--
cobp0106	Peter M: Yeah, it was.
cobp0107	Tim: It was a mob-run deal.
cobp0108	Peter M: That's true.
cocc0101	Lleslle: So Tim created a character which I very much appreciate. I love her. Her name is Lupe, and she happens to be a little bit based on me and my idiosyncrasies. And so when he--I remember one time we were hanging out, and he told me, he's like, "See, how can you tell me that I don't understand our relationship? Like, I can write about it." And I was like, "What are you talking about?" And he said, "See, she's super organized, nobody listens to her, but she has a lot of really important things to say. And, see, we need her! And that's what you've realized."
cocc0102	Darragh: That's awesome.
cocc0103	Lleslle: So I was like, "Oh, I love you." And she does karate kicks. That was the best 'cause I like to punch people. And so if you notice sometimes--I don't know if they have it in here--but she, uh, she punches in the air. She does air punches.
cocc0104	Clint: That's awesome.
cocc0105	Darragh: She was really easy to cast because I had such a great frame of reference for her.
cocc0106	Lleslle: Oh, nice. Thank you!
cocc0201	Tim: These scenes are from the production of the game in a way, where Lleslle would come to me like, "Look what I made! I made a chart of every single scene in the game, plus every single actor, and I coded it. Yellow means Manny's in it, and blue means this prop is in it," and she was so excited about these things that she did, and this character's doing the exact thing.
cocc0202	Peter M: Yup.
cocc0203	Tim: You have to take things from your life and put them in your work, you know. Even though they don't really mean anything to anybody else.
cocc0204	Peter M: Well, then that just means work will be about life, rather than life being about work, right?
cocc0205	Tim: Exactly, exactly.
codh0101	Paul: No, and I think this is one of my first sets. Oh, yeah! I remember this is-- Both of those were... Well, it was one of the first [environments] that I ever modeled. And again, it was very simple. Very simple geometry.
colm0101	Darragh: That's Kay Kuter. And Kay was, I don't know, late seventies, early eighties. Um, and big long gray beard... And he was a raging smoker. So you'd have to break him every forty-five minutes, otherwise he'd start getting into a really bad mood. And on one of those sessions, we were doing-- He plays the croupier later on, where he speaks French and English. It's just, he delivers the numbers. We were behind on time and so I sort of pushed him to an hour, and all of a sudden I found myself in an argument with Kay over how to pronounce "twenty" in French. And so I was like, "It's definitely time for a smoke break. [I'll just go] and get out of here."
cosd0101	Darragh: That's Raphael Sbarge, who, um-- He was actually the main character in "Knights of the Old Republic," in the original game. But Raphael, he's a big on-camera guy. Hadn't really done a lot of voice-over. And there's a scene later on where he's addressing all of the workers. And the thing about Raphael is that he physically performs when he's in the studio. So as he was addressing the field he kept on going off mic. So the engineer's like, "Oh, we gotta keep him on mic." So I said, "Hey, Raphael. You know, can you stay on mic?" He's like, "Oh! Really sorry, really sorry." We re-rolled and all of a sudden his energy just went [plummeting sound]... down."
cosd0102	Julian: Oh, interesting.
cosd0103	Darragh: It just wasn't working the same way, so we pulled out another two mics and put them out.
cosd0104	Julian: Oh, that's great!
cosd0105	Darragh: He could then go, you know, "You are the fat cats of industry!" and he could kind of move his head, and we still caught it. So then we just cut around, um, the three tracks to get the final take."
cosd0106	Julian: Oh, that's so crazy.
cosd0107	Darragh: Yeah... He's a great actor.
cosd0201	Tim: This is where-- I had never heard of actual Seabees before, and someone made that-- I talked about how there could be these bees down there. And they're like, "Oh, like Seebees!" And I was like, "What? What are Seabees?"
cosd0202	Peter M: Yeah, they were a Navy thing.
cosd0203	Tim: And, wait, does that come from the letters CB? like...?
cosd0204	Peter M: I don't know.
cosd0205	Tim: "Construction brigade" or something?
cosd0206	Peter M: Like Jeep comes from "General Personnel Vehicle," yeah.
cosd0207	Tim: So, and this bee is called Terry because the character from "On the Waterfront"--Marlon Brando's character named "Terry." Um, so that's where we got all that stuff.
code0101	Alex: But I do remember-- Did you guys notice, or do you remember this, that you couldn't solve this puzzle if you had a fast computer?
code0102	Paul: Really?
code0103	Alex: Yeah so right after the game shipped computers, you know, got better, right? And so the processors were much, much faster. And there's a timing element to this puzzle. And if your computer was too fast there was not enough time to actually execute and do the puzzle.
code0104	Dave: That's pretty awesome.
code0105	Alex: Yeah, so that was a bummer.
code0106	Dave: And not awesome.
code0201	Tim: This was our first 3-D game, and it's funny to see how many puzzles are about the physical properties of things. As opposed to, in a 2-D adventure game you would never make something based on the shape of something, or leverage. Like that whole puzzle was very physical, like you had to move these two objects and stop them from sliding past each other, and the fork lift jacked up on its back wheels. I think we were just so excited to be in a world of physical stuff, or maybe because we were in a world of physical stuff we had to actually make it physically realistic.
coev0101	Paul: I think we're going to come up to Gaurav's set that I destroyed-- That I destroyed two weeks of Gaurav's work, um, by trying to learn how to 3-D model. And somehow I ended up erasing two weeks of his work. It was the fish place that you had to redo.
coev0102	Gaurav: Oh yeah, the elevator!
coev0103	Paul: Yeah, the elevator and that place with the fish.
cose0101	Bret: To some extent, "Grim Fandango" is a bit of a mutt. It's got pieces of "Jedi Knight," it's got pieces of SCUMM, it's got pieces of "Outlaws." It's got pieces of, you know it's got iMUSE coming in, it's got the Lua language. So it was a game kind of made-- You know for all the technical challenges in it, a lot of pieces were sort of pre-existing, and kind of, it was a matter maybe of a new era of game development where you don't write everything from scratch, but you kind of put it together in the best possible way.
cose0102	Kevin: Well, and one of the side effects of that was since we had pretty robust solutions to video playback and rendering and art pipes in front of us, it really, a lot of the effort--aside from just making everything, all the blocking and tackling working--was spent in on how to make making games like this convenient and producible and artist-friendly, and uh... You know, you had built some tools that were really unlike the things that appeared in Renderdroid or Smush or some of the technologies it was built on. So it's a bit of a mutt in that we borrowed a bunch of really great technology, but there was also a lot of really innovative stuff.
cohl0101	Julian: Well, I remember--speaking of VO--I remember you casting this, and then I remember you vanishing. And you were gone for so long, recording this.
cohl0102	Darragh: Yeah, I was down in-- Actually, it was the one time we rented an apartment rather than stay at a hotel. I was down in LA recording this for six weeks, and then I was book-ended on either side with two weeks for two Lucas Learning projects. And so it was, uh...
cohl0103	Julian: Yeah, you just moved away.
cohl0104	Darragh: Yeah, yeah. It was a long time.
cohl0201	Mark: Is that cat actually modeled, or is that a painting?
cohl0202	Paul: No, that's a modeled cat. That's a--
cohl0203	Mark: The lighting is so soft, it's...
cohl0204	Paul: I think I might've even-- Oh, please, please, did I do that? Did I model that cat? I might've modeled that cat.
cohl0205	Mark: It's a cool-looking cat, just the lighting--
cohl0206	Paul: Oh, thank you.
cohl0207	Dave: [with accent] Is one sexy cat.
cohl0301	Tim: Wait, why did-- How did Glottis leave the piano-playing thing and come down here? Oh, you got him the pass. You gave him a pass.
cohl0302	Peter M: Right, and now he's a high roller.
cohl0303	Tim: And he's an alcoholic and a gambler because of you. But it's all for a good cause: adventure game-playing.
colx0101	Tim: I mean, that was always my favorite piece of concept art, was the flowers flying off the lighthouse.
colx0102	Peter M: Yes!
colx0103	Tim: This lighthouse is always so beautiful.
colx0104	Peter M: Yeah.
colx0105	Tim: And, um, a lot of those noir stories just have these really hard luck characters who don't get a break. And you know, the private eye's trying to solve the story, but they'll meet someone along the way who just gets bumped off and it's not their fault, and they just get wrapped up in things and--
colx0106	Peter M: Yeah, like the little two-time crook in--or two-bit crook--in, uh, "The Big Sleep," who drinks the poison but doesn't spill the beans, as it were, yeah.
colx0107	Tim: And Lola's one of those, um--
colx0108	Peter M: Lola's one of those characters.
colx0109	Tim: -- those victims. But you get to learn a lot!
colx0110	Peter M: Who's faithful but down and out.
colx0201	Bret: So all the water in year two was a mess. And I was actually very unhappy about the water because they loved making realistic-looking water, and they wanted to use all these shaders in Softimage to make the water look awesome. Have waves and everything. And so there's all these scenes, like this lighthouse scene, where there's like huge amounts of the screen are just undulating water, which compresses terribly for Smush and video. It was just awful. And that's all through this part of year two and I kept-- I got so freaked out because I thought that we'd never be able to do anything if we're just having every background-- We might as we all make every background be a full running movie at this rate.
colx0202	Kevin: Yeah, it was like for the film industry--where Softimage was mostly being used--water was kind of the soup du jour. It was-- Everybody was doing water.
colx0203	Bret: "How good is your water?"
colx0204	Kevin: Yeah, and so you would fire up Softimage and it could do all this amazing water simulation.
colx0205	Bret: I remember arguing with Peter [Tsaykel] about it, 'cause Pete wanted to have, you know, these angles where you would see a bunch of the water. Like, "You can't do it! Don't do it." He's like, "No, you've got to be able to see the water." And then we'd have like this, you know, three-quarters of the screen was just this undulating water. And they're big, you know, they're movies and they're big and awesome.
colx0206	Kevin: So there you can see some of the artifacts on the spotlight.
colx0207	Bret: Yeah.
colx0208	Kevin: Where it gets all bandy. That's just a--
colx0209	Bret: 16-bit, baby.
colx0210	Kevin: Yeah, a result of the video compression, and the water would do that.
colx0211	Bret: Yeah, you can see the frame rate on the background doesn't match, I think because we tried to make that one smaller. That lighthouse is just such a mess. We actually made it smaller. I think we ran at a lower frame just so we could actually fit it in memory.
colx0301	Tim: It goes from the super traumatic moment of the flowers and her death, and then all of a sudden there's this tongue on a card. The numbers are important, that's all I'm going to say. But "lengua"-- Around this time I went to a, um-- I remember going to a festival, a street festival. Carneval festival in The Mission. And drinking a lot of beer, and then I think the more drunk you are in the sunshine at a festival, the more likely you are to eat tongue tacos. That's my personal theory. So I ordered, um-- I was like, "What's a 'lengua'?" And they're like, "It's tongue." I was like, "Give it to me!" And it was delicious.
colx0302	Peter M: Of course it is!
colx0303	Tim: Super tender.
colx0304	Peter M: I was going to say... See, you're talking to the wrong guy.
colx0305	Tim: Delicious. I liked it so much that we put the "lengua" in here. So good. I recommend everyone eat some tongue.
colx0306	Peter M: Yeah, I mean be consistent here.
colx0307	Tim: It doesn't lick you back, if that's what you're worried about.
colx0308	Peter M: Exactly, and it's--
colx0309	Tim: You think it's going to be all tough, like your own tongue.
colx0310	Peter M: No, no.
colx0311	Tim: And you hate biting your own tongue, but...
colx0312	Peter M: No... There is a little bit of worry about biting someone else's tongue, that's true. There's something a little...
colx0313	Tim: Or that it's tasting you.
colx0314	Peter M: Yeah, yeah.
cohh0101	Alex: How big were those, when you drew them? Like the posters and the murals and stuff.
cohh0102	Paul: We could actually work rather big.
cohh0103	Dave: Did you have Photoshop back then?
cohh0104	Paul: We had Photoshop, but you... I don't know if we had layers back then.
cohh0105	Gaurav: Oh, we had layers.
cohh0106	Paul: We did-- Oh we had layers, but maybe only--
cohh0107	Dave: Paul just didn't know what they were.
cohh0108	Paul: No! But I think, too, you only had one undo, or maybe two undos.
cohh0109	Mark: Oh, wow.
cocn0101	Paul: Oh, wow! I forgot about that mural. That was supposed to be--
cocn0102	Alex: Wow, that's really good.
cocn0103	Paul: Oh, well, thank you. I did that.
cocn0104	Alex: Did you?
cocn0105	Paul: Uh, no I did. But it was based on, you know, like a Gustav Klimt. But then I also did those really bad textures on the roulette wheels, and uh...
cocn0106	Dave: Oh, they're fine.
cocn0107	Paul: Oh... But I remember Adam Schnitzer actually getting angry at me. He was like, "Don't paint light in the paintings."
cocn0108	Dave: Who got angry with you?
cocn0109	Paul: Oh, not really angry at me. He only--
cocn0110	Dave: It probably felt the same way as when Kim said, er, Tim said, "Manny's not cool enough sliding down the banister! Just make it cool... Canadian animator."
cocn0111	Paul: Heh, what's that aboot?
cocn0112	Dave: That's now so not cool.
cocn0201	Tim: Chowchilla Charlie.
cocn0202	Peter M: Oh, yes.
cocn0203	Tim: That name comes from an actual person I know who was from Chowchilla, which no one remembers anymore. But when I was a kid, an entire school bus full of children was kidnapped in the town of Chowchilla. Do you remember that? In California--
cocn0204	Peter M: No, wow.
cocn0205	Tim: A school bus full of kids was kidnapped, and the people demanded ransom. And it was crazy to think about. It was on the news, it was... Chowchilla, everyone was talking about these kids. And eventually they were found and rescued, and I don't believe they were hurt, so I can talk about that story now. And all the kids went home. But, um, and then years later I just, um, a friend of ours was dating this guy named Charlie, and he was from Chowchilla. And I was like, "Oh wow, were you on that bus?" He's like, "No, I was sick that day. I was sick, I didn't go to school that day, and so I didn't get kidnapped."
cocn0206	Peter M: Wow.
cocn0207	Tim: We always referred to him as "Chowchilla Charlie," and he's now in the game as Chowchilla Charlie, even though his personality is not as sleazy as this character. He's a fine, outstanding--although very lucky--guy.
cocn0208	Peter M: Yeah.
cocn0209	Tim: But this is our Peter Lorre character.
cocn0301	Peter M: Manny's music--the "Casino Calavera" music--is uh, that was a big moment for me doing the score because it was done fairly early on. And it kind of, it determined a lot of other stuff. Just sort of stylistically. Get that sort of Gene Krupa thing going on in the beginning, and--
cocn0302	Tim: Gene Krupa?
cocn0303	Peter M: Yeah, one of the great drummers of all time.
cocn0401	Tim: I always wanted to have a really international flare. I guess because "Casablanca" was very international, but I also love the game, "Last Express," the Jordan Mechner game. "Last Express": you're on a train and there's a lot of listening to conversations that are not presented at full volume, because you're eavesdropping. You're walking down the hallway and you hear someone talking in a room. But they would often be very quiet, but also sometimes in another language, like French, or German or something. And just the idea of, like, overhearing a quietly spoken French conversation was so neat in that game that we tried to have people talking in other languages in here. Like the croupier in the game where he's only speaking French, I believe.
cocf0101	Tim: So this is one of the parts, um, in the game that was very inspired by "Gilda." You ever seen "Gilda?" Um, I think Rita Hayworth.
cocf0102	Peter M: No.
cocf0103	Tim: Uh, it's a great noir movie--
cocf0104	Peter M: I'm just going to go home and see it now.
cocf0105	Tim: It's amazing, and it's the scene that you always see when they show Rita Hayworth, where she just flips her hair up and says, "Who, me?" Have you ever seen that...? So anyway, it's about a, um-- It's set in a resort town where there's a casino, and the main character... There is a... The main character's working in a casino and there's this kind of corrupt local law enforcement officer who shows up every night. And the way he gets paid off by the casino, to not bust it, is he always comes and bets on number two at a certain table. And then they always give him his money, and number two always wins and stuff. And so that, I think it's the same number in this game. We always use number two.
cocf0201	Peter M: When we leave the diner for the last time, there's a sort of a moment with the-- Just kind of a technical and sort of dramatic moment with the music where the radio, the music on the radio is playing and there's going to be a little bit of a cut and then there's-- You go to this cutscene music, which takes... It's supposed to be sort of a seamless break from the game music to the cutscene music. And then the music kind of comes out of the radio and it's not, doesn't sound like it's on a radio anymore. It's the music of people's, you know, dreams and wishes.
comx0101	Tim: Rubacava is named after a girl I knew in the fourth grade. Her last name was Rulbacava. There's a fun fact for you.
comx0102	Peter M: Wow.
comx0103	Tim: She's married now. Doesn't use it anymore, so I get to use it.
comx0104	Peter M: You're keeping it alive.
comx0105	Tim: Mm hm.
comg0101	Dave: How was the lighting handled on the characters in each environment?
comg0102	Dave: Does anybody remember that?
comg0103	Peter T: I think, uh, however we were able to set up a couple of lights. I think that was it.
comg0104	Paul: Yeah, because wasn't there some information, that you guys would take the set-- Because didn't we have to save a copy of our sets with, like, the key light and the fill. Or just-- I thought we would save a version of the very simple...
comg0105	Peter T: That's possible, yeah. As like a starting place, but I mean it was all very kind of rudimentary.
comg0106	Dave: And you had to be really strategic with, you know what is the best use of a minimal amount of lights to accommodate the character in the entire scene that he's got to be in.
comg0107	Peter T: There was no-- I mean for all this stuff, there was no road map or procedure for like, oh, this is how you do 3-D forests, or light characters. I mean we had to make everything up and solve every problem to get everything to happen pretty much in this-- So there's a lot of just on-the-fly, different people figuring stuff out per scene, how to make something work. And as a result, some things work better than others.
comg0201	Tim: Oh, Membrillo... My brother actually makes "membrillo," which is a quince jelly that you eat with cheese. "Membrillo" means quince. Why are you looking at me like that?
comg0202	Peter M: Sure doesn't sound like it.
comg0203	Tim: "Membrillo."
comg0204	Peter M: Ah, "membrillo!" That's different.
comg0205	Tim: Yeah. What did you think I said?
comg0206	Peter M: [laughter]
cohp0101	Tim: Here we're being very insensitive to claustrophobics. And French people. Probably would never do it today.
cohp0102	Peter M: Well, the Twitter-- Just think of the Twitter response from the claustrophobic French people, right?
cohp0103	Tim: Mm hm.
copc0101	Bret: Okay, I remember at the time it was a big debate. Like, "Should we require P100 or P133?" Like, Pentium 100, Pentium 133. It's like, "Nope, your target platform is whatever was on sale at CompUSA for five hundred bucks last year."
copc0102	Kevin: It's like a P70.
copc0103	Bret: Yeah, it's like what the-- You know P60 I think is what we ended up with as the minimum spec. We ended up going for P90 at the end. But it was one of those things where it's like those kind of constraints, where it's like you really could pull it off with all the kinds of machines we had in the company, but the average consumer who we wanted to sell the game to did not have a machine like that. That's one of the vagaries of the PC market that still persists.
copc0104	Kevin: Yeah, and you wouldn't, you know when you think about "Grim" you wouldn't necessarily think, but there's like all hand-coded assembly [programming language] rasterizers in here. There's hand-coded assembly blitters in here. Um, lots of, like, layout asset management. This was a-- This was a tricky game to optimize and to get running acceptably on the kind of machines that people were going to be running it on. But it doesn't come off like the same technical challenge as a "Jedi Knight" or something like that. And it wasn't.
copc0105	Bret: Different kind of technical challenge. Much more, kind of iterative.
copc0106	Kevin: It was really getting all this weird stuff happening at the same time.
copi0101	Dave: I always remember when finishing an animation, you know I'd show it to Pete [Tsaykel] or something, and he'd be like, "Yeah, that's cool." And then we'd always have to show it to Tim. And Tim's feedback... was interesting. He always-- I don't know whether he likes them, I never knew whether he liked something or not, but it always ended with some kind of joke, or sarcastic comment. Which was always fun.
copi0102	Paul: I remember one time showing Pete something. He said, "Well, I don't like it, but I don't know why." And... Which is the most helpful criticism that you can ever get, um, because you don't know how to fix the thing. But I'm being facetious.
copi0201	Tim: We wanted them to show-- Eventually, you're going to see him do a bee dance, which we were really proud to get. I had to actually do some dancing for the animators to get that to... be accurate as possible. 'cause you know when bees find pollen, or a bunch of flowers, they fly back to the hive and then they shake their abdomens in a certain way which tells people what-- It helps leads the other bees.
copi0202	Peter M: That's right, yeah.
copi0203	Tim: So when Terry is trying to lead the, um-- This right here, he's like trying to lead the other bees, and so he gives them a speech and then he shakes his abdomen a little bit to, uh, tell everybody where to go. Shake shake shake--I can do that dance really well.
cosi0101	Clint: One thing for a sound designer, for a composer. When the story is great and the writing is great, and the pacing is great--whether it's a film or a game--you can't help but weigh in in a successful way yourself because there is this flow already, and you're just participating with it and enhancing it. One of the jobs of a composer especially-- In a game like this, a lot of time passes and you need to entertain the listener and provide musical interest, but it has to also last for a very long time without being tiresome. And I think Pete did a great job at striking a balance between a great deal of musical interest but also kind of hanging back and taking its time and letting the story unfold. Letting the player concentrate on the game.
cosi0201	Tim: And uh, so that guy--Toto Santos, which is the name of the All Souls' Day--that was one important casting thing that meant so much at the time, and um, I love "The Usual Suspects."
cosi0202	Peter M: Yes.
cosi0203	Tim: There's a burn victim at the very beginning of the [film], a guy who was burned on a ship.
cosi0204	Peter M: Right.
cosi0205	Tim: And he's in the bed, and he's the first guy who says, "Keyser Söze."
cosi0206	Peter M: Got it, right.
cosi0207	Tim: He's this Hungarian or Polish guy. And we got that actor to do Toto Santos's voice, 'cause I was like, "I want him to sound like that guy from 'The Usual Suspects!'" And so if you listen to him he's just--
cosi0208	Peter M: That's awesome.
cosi0209	Tim: I just, I wanted to have him say, "Keyser Söze," in the game, but he doesn't.
cosi0301	Lleslle: I love how all the characters have very distinct voices that match their personality and their look. Like, how much would Tim give you direction and how much was your direction, um, influencing?
cosi0302	Darragh: Well, ideally when you go through the process you um, you know you have character pictures along with the audition copy and we would send that out to the various agencies, and the auditions would come back. It kind of, the process kind of does that for you because you're listening to the auditions and you're looking at the picture and you narrow it down to like, you know, a handful of candidates and then sort of, we'd sit down with Tim and... You know, just go through them and sometimes there's only one choice. You just know it.
cosi0303	Lleslle: Right. It's just so... great. All of them.
cosi0304	Darragh: What you do is you cast for like the major characters and then use that same talent pool with a lot of the smaller characters. And so that's why Kay [Kuter] is, you know he was cast for Velasco, but then the croupier just was one of those that's like, okay, well, that fits. He works.
cosi0305	Lleslle: So you let the actors pretty much kind of come up with their character as well, when they look at the picture?
cosi0306	Darragh: Yeah, yeah. Like for those secondary characters, they're totally undefined when you're in the studio and so you just kind of sit there, look at the picture... Look at the lines, talk about it, dial a voice in. And then go.
cosi0307	Lleslle: Yeah, just create... Ah, I love it! It's just creative everywhere, you know? Like through the whole thing. Not just with us but even with the actors and stuff like that. You really are like watching a movie. It's amazing.
cosi0308	Darragh: You really want that relationship between sort of the voice-over director and the actor to be collaborative and allow them the freedom to bring stuff to the table, and try things and, um, it just adds a lot.
cosl0101	Darragh: This is Pamela Segall, who plays Bobby on "King of the Hill." She's quite a character. She has a minivan with flames on the side.
cosl0102	Julian: Oh, yes.
cosl0103	Darragh: And a skull on the, uh, the front where it replaces-- I don't know what brand it is, but let's say it's Chrysler or somebody. And so instead of the Chrysler logo there's a skull and crossbones. She's pretty funny.
cosl0104	Julian: Did she just do this as one big long...?
cosl0105	Darragh: Yeah, all one take so she could build and build.
cosl0106	Julian: Yeah, yeah.
cosl0107	Darragh: You know, let it sort of ebb and flow.
cosl0108	Clint: And obviously, Pete's [McConnell] quotation of "There's No Place Like Home" is something we all admired in Carl Stalling, who did a lot of the compositions for the "Merrie Melodies" Looney Tunes cartoons. And any time, as you may know, that a chicken comes on screen or something it will play "Turkey in the Straw," or you know various public domain songs that were relevant to the subject of the moments, so she was reminiscing about home and Pete strikes up a--
cosl0109	Julian: That's really cool.
cosl0110	Clint: "There's No Place Like Home."
cosl0201	Tim: Her um, her character's a little bit inspired by, I think it's "Big Sleep" too, where Bogart is hiding from someone. He needs to hide in a book store.
cosl0202	Peter M: Right.
cosl0203	Tim: And he goes in there, and there's a woman in there and she's really into him.
cosl0204	Peter M: "Do you have a Ben Hur, 1863 edition, the one with the errata on page... 47 or something?"
cosl0205	Tim: Wow, that is a memory you have.
cosl0206	Peter M: Well...
cosl0207	Tim: And it's just really-- He just, he looks at her and he's like, um, she seems like she's really attracted to him, if I remember correctly. And he like takes off her glasses, he's like, "Yeah, okay." It's a really kind of messed up scene.
cosl0208	Peter M: Well, that's-- You know, that was the subtext of the noir film.
cosl0209	Tim: And then he pulled the shade.
cosl0210	Peter M: Hanky panky.
cosl0211	Tim: Yeah, the hanky panky. Yeah they lock the door and pull down the shades. And that's a little bit what this scene is a reference to, or inspired by.
cosl0212	Peter M: 'cause she's telling him her story.
cosl0213	Tim: Mm hm.
cogt0101	Clint: Speaking of database, I mean that was really how we accomplished so much, right? Is from an organizational standpoint of the music, of the sound effects, and of the voice. And I remember, especially for voice, how critical it was. And what we in the sound department would do is inherit and share the use of the database with the voice department, um, and you could do a search and isolate all the lines for, say, Manny, or isolate all the lines that needed to have reverb baked into them, or another type of processing. And then you would just divide and conquer, and just master everything in groups and bring it all together. Have single reference for volume. And uh, use the database to keep organized and isolate different found sets of different criteria.
cots0101	Tim: Strange, the characters in this game don't like to go into crowded areas, almost as if it's too hard to render them.
cobi0101	Bret: There's that spotlight.
cobi0102	Kevin: Mm hm. Yeah, so this was another like-- We lifted up the, er, took the technology from Renderdroid, which was in "Jedi Knight," but it didn't have spotlights at the time. It just had directional lights and point lights. So that was actually one of the very first things when, uh--
cobi0103	Bret: Yeah, that's the first thing you did.
cobi0104	Kevin: Yeah, when I got hired onto the team, which I was brand new to LucasArts, and uh, Tim and Bret brought me in and my first thing I was like, "I'm gonna put in spotlights, 'cause I know how to do that, and it's gonna matter, and uh, that'll be my stake in the ground." And this was one of the first scenes I worked on that had spotlights. So... when Manny walks off stage and you see the light, you know when he walks out of the light... That was a pretty important moment. Everybody thought that was pretty cool. I was like, alright, I did something. I'm in this amazing project with this amazing team and I did something.
cobi0201	Tim: And then, for some reason I was always really interested in beatniks. Beatniks and reds. I think it was so foreign to me, like the "Red Scare" was not part of my actual life, but it always seemed like-- This thing is, it's hard... It was always funny to me to imagine how passionate people were about that. How big of a deal--
cobi0202	Peter M: Oh yeah.
cobi0203	Tim: -- it was for them. And it was always... from a safe distance it was just kind of entertaining. Like, oh wow, people were really concerned about Communists everywhere and unions and...
cobi0204	Peter M: I remember there were--when I was a little kid there were-- people would still talk about Communist plots in all seriousness, yeah.
cobe0101	Paul: Oh and I'm sorry, Gaurav, this is the set that you had to build twice.
cobe0102	Mark: This is Gaurav's, right?
cobe0103	Gaurav: Yeah, this is the one.
cobe0104	Paul: This was the gift that kept on giving.
cobe0105	Dave: And it's actually very beautiful.
cobe0106	Paul: Yes! Well, a second time is...
cobe0107	Gaurav: Too kind. Yeah, the second time...
cobe0108	Paul: But I'm sorry, you were saying that that was a real building.
cobe0109	Gaurav: Yeah, yeah.
cobe0110	Paul: Where? Was that in Spain?
cobe0111	Gaurav: Uh, I think it's in Paris. I think. And I remember seeing a photo of it and being like, "Wait, Peter [Tsaykel]..."
cobe0112	Paul: Uh, nuh... Good artists borrow, great artists steal.
cobw0101	Paul: Oh, we definitely should give a huge shout-out to Peter Chan and-- Yeah, I'm still jealous of that guy.
cobw0102	Dave: "Chan the Man."
cobw0103	Paul: I know. Just when those packages [of concept art] would come it would just be like Christmas, and you'd open... Yeah you'd see those and you just think, "Aw man, and he's a nice guy, too," you know. Happy family--
cobw0104	Dave: The weird thing about that was I never experienced like the delivery of the package first-hand, but I still got excited about it.
cobw0105	Paul: Nah, they were beautiful. And he's such a sweetheart.
coci0101	Dave: I remember animating Manny sliding down the banister. And I remember Tim kept calling for something... "He's gotta look cool!" And so I did that, and I don't think he was ever really happy with it. So I don't know, apparently I don't know what "cool" means.
coci0102	Mark: Gotta put some Ray-Bans on him.
coci0103	Dave: Aw, yeah. I mean he's, Manny's a suave guy but I don't know if suave guys slide down banisters. Maybe I should have done it slower.
coci0201	Tim: You get a special award for playing the "Rusty Anchor" song.
coci0202	Peter M: I secretly wanted to be, to sing this one. I did a demo for you, remember?
coci0203	Tim: Yeah, this is one of the things--much like Cabbage Boy--that we wrote together. I wrote the lyrics and then you came up with a tune and then changed the lyrics a little bit to actually make them more musical... "My boat's got a rusty anchor..."
coci0204	Peter M: Yeah... "Oh, rusty anchor! Going down, down, down." See?
coci0205	Tim: Do it! "Oh, rusty anchor... [Guess I gotta] stink around." I said "stink around." It's "stick around."
coci0301	Tim: I wish you would slide down the banister because we put a lot of effort into sliding that banister.
coci0302	Tim: Come on, slide down the banister. I recommend any player who's in this room slide down the banister. It's much faster than walking.
coci0303	Peter M: Especially if you're six and you don't have terrible consequences when you hit the bottom.
coci0304	Tim: You should not try that at home. Actually, kids, you should. You should go slide down the banister of your house right now.
coci0305	Peter M: Everybody's gotta do it once!
cowc0101	Bret: So this is a-- This is one of the parts of the game where a huge amount was cut out. There was supposed to be a gigantic action sequence, like there was in all of-- You know, I think we cut a giant action sequence out of every year. But there was a gigantic action sequence where there was an animal called the "Dillopede" that guarded the bottom of the elevator. It was a giant armadillo. And the Dillopede was there to, uh, kind of snap at Manny as he came down. There's this whole sequence where you had to kind of, you know... You had the Dillopede hanging by some elevator cable and he was snapping at you, and you had to swing on him or something like that, and... It's just the idea of a giant, vicious armadillo still sticks with me, but it was one of those things where it's like, you look at the context of the game and it's an adventure game and the budget's short. We were already going to be a huge game. What purpose is this serving the game? So we had to cut a lot of stuff out like that, that just was really cool and fun to design but ultimately not critical to the game and was going to be really expensive.
#year3commentary	
cofh0101	Dave: Tim had the coolest office.
cofh0102	Mark: He did.
cofh0103	Dave: It was filled with, like, tiki lights and it felt like, it was like Christmas land. Not Christmasey, but... It gave you that same feeling of decoration and homeyness.
cofh0104	Alex: Yeah. I was going to say warmth. Yeah, homeyness. There was a couch in there which was always nice.
cofh0105	Dave: Yup. And I think I probably overstayed my welcome in there a few times.
cofh0106	Gaurav: I think we all probably did.
cofh0107	Mark: You never knew when you were, when you should leave 'cause it was so much fun in there, but then it's like, oh man, he has work to do.
cofh0108	Dave: Yeah, it's no wonder he had to work through the night. It's because we would--
cofh0109	Alex: We would bother him all the time.
cofh0110	Dave: His office was like going to Disneyland and you'll just sit in there and talk on the couch and just want to be a part of this... uh... this creation.
cofh0111	Alex: He had some old game consoles in there, and I would go and talk classic games with him 'cause he knew that I was an enthusiast as well. So that was fun.
cofh0112	Mark: I think he should have made his office more boring.
cofh0113	Dave: He did spend a lot of time in that office.
cofh0114	Alex: How's his office now?
cofh0115	Dave: I've seen pictures. I think it's pretty cool.
cofh0116	Mark: It's pretty cool. It's like a grown-up version of that office, maybe. He's still got all his Rubik's Cubes on his desk.
cofh0117	Paul: Oh, I forgot about the--
cofh0118	Dave: I forgot he was a master of the Rubik's Cube.
cofh0119	Mark: He is.
cofh0120	Alex: Really?
cofh0121	Mark: Yes. Don't challenge him.
cofh0122	Alex: Well, I would never.
cofo0101	Tim: Mm hm. I always thought it was because they're so cute, I just had to, um, be mean to them. Like once, when my sister got her tonsils taken out, she had big puffy cheeks. I remember my aunt came to visit and she goes, "Oh, you look so cute! I just want to be mean to you!" And I never really understood it until I, we had these little baby angels in the game. I just, "Ooh, I just, wanna just terrorize them!" So you can get out your scythe and scare them and do all these horrible things.
cofo0102	Peter M: It's the Mr. Bill--
cofo0103	Tim: It's a little sick.
cofo0104	Peter M: It's the Mr. Hands in all of us.
cofo0201	Tim: "Angelitos" is actually, um... The little children in the cage, comes from a term that-- It was a popular kind of, um... Well, it was traditional in Mexico to take pictures of your children, even if they died very young. Like, photography of little dead children.
cofo0202	Peter M: It's a old genre, yeah.
cofo0203	Tim: I don't know if that happened in the States, but it was definitely in Mexico.
cofo0204	Peter M: Mexico, yeah. I've seen those. I think you showed them to me, actually.
cofo0205	Tim: And the word they use for children when they-- When they're young, when they died is "angelitos."
cofo0206	Peter M: Oh, yeah.
cofo0207	Tim: It's a dark, dark game.
cofo0208	Peter M: It is dark.
cocy0101	Tim: God, this is where people-- Nowadays it would be like, "Tim, the ideas you have here about this conveyor belt where they can switch directions and rip those chains with a scoop is really complicated, and we could eliminate a lot of work if we just cut this one puzzle." But it's just like, no, we went all the way and we fully implemented exactly what I hoped would happen. The conveyor belt would reverse direction, chain would go up and down, Manny would climb down and against the force of water, bunch up the chain. This is all so complicated. Oh, and you create a little loop. I was like, "This all makes sense in my mind, so the player will be able to figure this out." Like the way that you looped it over that, um, boat. I don't even know if that would work. That's like when you whip a hose to like get it uncaught, when you're watering the plants in the backyard. Your hose gets caught.
cocy0102	Peter M: Right, right, yup. And it did a little whiplash effect, yeah.
cocv0101	Kevin: So one of the things that was super-- Another technical problem that was really interesting was the save/load, 'cause I wanted to cut a bunch of corners on save/load to just make it a little easier to implement. And we had talked about things like you could only save and load, or when you load it would load at the beginning of a room and back up content a little bit. Or just do it where it was a little bit more convenient. And Tim really wanted a really high-fidelity save/load. I remember trying to talk him out of it and he threw the gauntlet down. He was like, "If Manny is in the middle of a line and there's a puff of smoke hanging in the air, then, and I hit save, when he restores he should pick up from the middle of that line where he was at and that puff of smoke should be in the air." And I was like, "Alright, man, if that's what you want that's what you're gonna get." And uh, it was a big pain. It took a lot of work to get it there. But that's the way the game shipped and that's kind of, you know, with controls and things like that, Tim was pretty uncompromising and had a vision of what he wanted this thing to be. And, you know, as much as we were able we gave him what he wanted.
cocu0101	Tim: Now this, all that water was running in the "Rebel Assault" engine. The game is running in the "Jedi Knight" engine, and the, uh--
cocu0102	Peter M: Oh, that's right!
cocu0103	Tim: All the streaming video is from the "Rebel Assault" engine.
cocu0104	Peter M: "... Assault" engine, that is right!"
cocu0105	Tim: So we're compositing the "Rebel Assault" technology on top of "Jedi Knight" technology, and then all the Lua scripting for the gameplay is what comprised the GrimE engine, which we never actually called the GrimE engine at the time but Bret wanted to call it the GrimE engine. It's like, "Grim."
cocu0106	Peter M: Oh, that's funny.
cocu0107	Tim: And later he's been able to do enough interviews where he's called it the GrimE engine, that people just call it the GrimE engine. Now that's fine, it should have a name.
cocu0108	Peter M: Everything should, yeah.
