cozw0101	Julian: You know, but hearing all these names, too, I just feel like a lot of times I'll catch myself saying, "I went there," instead of, "I worked there," because it was really like being-- It was an extension of college and it was just everyone. We were all friendly, we all hung out together, so whether we were hanging out at work or at someone's house, I mean it was just all a blur, you know?
cozw0102	Lleslle: That's funny, I went... "I went to LucasArts."
cozw0103	Julian: Yeah. Lucas High.
cozw0201	Tim: I think something that helped make this game--one of the secrets to it--was just watching a lot of old movies. Like a lot of this-- It's like some of them are references, but a lot of them are like, um, plot elements, ideas of relationships between people is the subtle stuff that came out of watching old black and white movies, 'cause they just did stuff so differently then, and every... I mean, I know there were formula movies back then but they're not the current formulas, you know, and they're not as pat as the movies you'd watch today. They're surprising, like old film noir movies had relationships between people that feel really different than modern relationships. Some of them are messed up and based on old social things, and some of them are just like, cooler? It's weird.
cozw0202	Peter M: Well, there's a certain stoicism that you don't... You don't...
cozw0203	Tim: And a certain--
cozw0204	Peter M: A kind of reserve, maybe, you don't see in characters now.
cozw0205	Tim: And a little bit of an honesty in characters, like nowadays you don't... I think the way that people talk now, there's like, there's different layers of meaning to everything, and different subtexts--
cozw0206	Peter M: Say "everything's cool," which means, you know... Which means, "eff you," you know, which means that... No, it's more like things were...
cozw0207	Tim: Even though they're old, they're very refreshing, is what I'm saying. Like "Casablanca" has a lot of just honest-- People talking honestly to each other in a way that's different than a modern movie.
cofi0101	Kevin: So yeah, one of the things that I think people don't realize is it's not just pre-rendered art, but there's pre-rendered z-buffers--
cofi0102	Bret: That's right.
cofi0103	Kevin: -- through the whole thing. And we thought that the pre-rendered z-buffers would just do all the work for us, and it turned out that--if you look through the game data--the pre-rendered z-buffers, there's a lot of hand=painted pre-rendered z-buffers. We made a z-buffer editing tool which was funky because you-- It's 16-bit data, and you can't edit 16-bit data in a 8-bit monitor, so...
cofi0104	Bret: It was grayscale. It looks like a ghost scene. It's like you look into like a foggy version of the world.
cofi0105	Kevin: Yeah, so we did like a quantized 8-bit version of it and you could paint into it. People would just use like big boxes and find places where he was clipping, or we didn't want him to clip. A lot of it was we wanted-- He was slightly behind something and he shouldn't have been, so he would like go into the edge of the sofa or something and you just black it out.
cofi0106	Bret: Just black it out, yeah.
cofi0107	Kevin: So that he would pop in front of it.
cofi0108	Bret: Yep.
cofi0201	Darragh: There's a character who shows up later on called The Florist, who is played by a great actor called Keith Szarabajka, who I've worked with a lot over the years and... Keith... Oftentimes, when he auditions the characters-- The way he does his auditions, it's not, they're not usually safe choices that he makes. And the first time I came across him was-- He was auditioning for Boston Low ["The Dig"] and he just... You know, Boston Low is a space commander, and Keith was doing something very... deep and dark and strange, and because his last name is Szarabajka, it just sort of stuck in my head and I used to always look forward to whatever audition Keith was gonna throw our way, and they were always bizarre and never appropriate. And then when he auditioned for The Florist, who's insane because he-- In the real world he's a florist and he deals with flowers, which are beautiful things, but in the "Grim Fandango" world it's death. He's gone crazy. And um... he's deranged. And Keith just nailed the part and was just brilliant and it was at the moment that I realized, oh, he's actually a normal dude and he can do all sorts of other things but just his audition choices are, you know are different and unusual.
cofi0301	Peter M: Yeah we used the sarangi, um, it's the Indian cello--
cofi0302	Tim: Oh, awesome.
cofi0303	Peter M: -- to sort of make a reference to the hippies, I guess you'd say.
cofi0304	Tim: Who was the... the guy with The Beatles?
cofi0305	Peter M: Uh, George Harrison--
cofi0306	Tim: No, the-- The guru they hung out with.
cofi0307	Peter M: Oh, the guru. The Maharishi, yeah. And also, um... Ravi Shankar was sort of a... friend of theirs. He was like a friend of theirs.
cofp0101	Tim: I really didn't like early 3-D, like nineties 3-D 'cause I always though it looked like cardboard boxes that were taped together and that someone had dragged nylons over, you know? And that implies a lot more smooth skinning than actually existed. It was really like-- And then everything was painted on the outside, even things that should be modeled as an interior space, like ribs on a skeleton or something. Everything was painted on the outside, and it looked ridiculous.
cofp0102	Peter M: So it looked like everybody was wearing a Halloween costume anyway.
cofp0103	Tim: Yeah! And then I saw those papier-mâché skeletons where they painted the ribs on the outside and I was like, "This is perfect. This kind of folk art would actually look really good in 3-D." There was a lot of pressure to get into 3-D back then 'cause it was seen as the future. I mean obviously it was 'cause a lot of, um, everything turned into 3-D, but 2-D is still a big money-making thing in games, too.
comy0101	Clint: You know, Lleslle, I remember how you worked to keep everyone together and to help the team snuff out bugs and get this thing finished up, and I have to confess something after all these years that as we would observe bugs in the game, that translated merely to us as, "Okay, fellas, we have more time. Let's get back to work. Let's make this stuff even better." And so we'd see someone's head float off their body and just exit the screen while their body continued to remain on screen, and we'd say, "That's at least another month."
comy0102	Lleslle: So that's how you got more time.
comy0103	Clint: We knew what the schedule was by just simply playing the game.
