come0313	Tim: Its themes are subtle.
come0401	Tim: I love how the audio here tells the story of the deadly chemicals moving up the pipes, and...
come0402	Peter M: So he kind of kills him from a distance. It's like the end of, uh... "Big Sleep."
come0403	Tim: I forgot that.
come0404	Peter M: Yeah. Shoots him from behind a car.
cohf0101	Tim: I do remember--during "Full Throttle"--you saying, "You know what we never made here is just a real, like, swing era, crime, film noir story." And it always kind of stuck with me 'cause I was like, "Yeah, we never have done one of those. We should do one because..." I don't know what started us-- A lot of us at the company were starting to get into film noir. I don't know if it was because there was a film festival at the Lark Theater, where they showed every single great noir film, and we went to that. Or if it was... just we were at that age where you start reading Raymond Chandler. We talked about Raymond Chandler a lot in the office, too. You were like, "She was so cold, a lump of spumoni wouldn't melt on her shoulder--"
cohf0102	Peter M: "Slice of spumoni wouldn't have melted on her shoulder," yeah.
cohf0103	Tim: Always stuck with me. I was like, "Man I gotta read more Raymond Chandler." So that started me on that.
cohf0104	Peter M: He's awesome. Yeah and during, it was either "Day of the Tentacle" or "Full Throttle," I wrote a little gangster theme just 'cause I thought it would be cool. It just popped into my head and I wanted to put the melody down, and that might have prompted that comment about it'd be cool if we did a noir thing someday.
cohf0201	Tim: I'm glad I put that in there. I didn't even know that happened. Manny actually got to do some salesmanship. Manny actually got to be a travel agent in the game. I've totally forgotten that.
cohf0202	Tim: Genius! Whoever made this is a genius.
cohf0203	Tim: That's what this commentary is all about. Just me watching things and being like, "Ah, dang. Good job."
cohf0204	Peter M: Yeah. Nice going, nice going.
conl0101	Bret: All we had to do was extract the text lines and just do what they'd always done for all the SCUMM games. But um, I had a brother who was a screenwriter in Hollywood, and he had all these special tools to format his scripts, you know as a screenplay. And I was like, "You know, it'd be really cool if I gave Darragh something he could bring into those guys who were really familiar with screenplays. He could bring them something that looked like a screenplay." So when I wrote the dialogue extraction I made it, um actually format it like a script, and actually did the character swing back and forth. And I gave Tim a way that he could indicate, in comments in the code-- If you format the comments a certain way, like you know, "she pouts," or, "huffily," or some other kind of cue, I would extract that and put it along with it. So just like in a normal screenplay you'd have like, you know, here's my line, "haughty" or whatever, you'd have some cue to the actor about how it's supposed to be read. And I even put in scene markers, like this is in the High Roller Casino, or this is exterior docks, so they had a context for where it was in the game. And uh, through the magic of HTML--the web was new--I was able to just spit out HTML, so that's what I did. I wrote a-- I made a screenplay-looking thing in HTML and then they were able to print out and take it down there.
cote0101	Kevin: I remember the, um, the big wall of concept art. There was like--
cote0102	Bret: Yeah, Chan art.
cote0103	Kevin: And fifteen feet tall. And you'll see on the Internet--probably see some of it on the special edition--of these pencil drawings that are-- And you really, you only get to see them one at a time, but they were hundreds of them, and they were all pinned up to this giant cork board.
cote0104	Bret: Yeah, it's like wallpaper.
cote0105	Kevin: They were like wallpaper, yeah. And it was amazing to see this beautiful art transform from these pencil drawings on the wall into the game in front of you.
cote0106	Bret: They kept the style in a lot of cases, I mean some of the things in the 3-D scenes were amazing.
cote0107	Kevin: Yeah, the faithfulness to those original Peter Chan drawings is incredible.
colw0101	Paul: Oh my God.
colw0102	Mark: Look at that.
colw0103	Paul: I forgot about this set.
colw0104	Mark: That looks like a painting or something, it's great.
colw0105	Paul: Yeah it, uh, this was one of the-- Yeah when you get a Peter Chan drawing, this one was a widow maker as well where you just thought, "How does that pipe go in that?"
colw0106	Gaurav: Things didn't quite line up.
colw0107	Dave: You know, I was gonna say that environment with the pipes in it, it almost had like a... a "Day of the Tentacle" or "Sam and Max" feel to it. You know, there's a lot distortion in the pipes and felt a little more wacky than what we see in the rest of the environments.
colw0108	Paul: No, definitely.
cosh0101	Darragh: I didn't put in too many late nights, but there was one where I was in the office at two in the morning, just making notes on the script, and all of a sudden Chuck Jordan walks by my office and says, "Hi!" And there was a way about him that-- Ten minutes later I got up from my desk. I went over to his office and went in and said, "Did you just get in?" And he's like, "Yeah. I get in at two." "Two in the morning?" He's like, "Yeah." And I guess that he'd put in so many late nights that his whole time clock had shifted and so he'd said to Tim, he said, "Hey is it okay if I just come in at two in the morning?" And Tim was like, "Sure!"
cosh0201	Tim: I really like puzzles where you use things backwards. Like you use the-- Instead of finding and using a metal detector to find something metal, you use something metal to find a metal detector in the kitty litter puzzle. And then here you're... Instead of, um... Sprouting, shooting sproutella into bones, you're using bone flakes to um... Expose a sproutella chain. I love this room! It looks almost exactly like the concept art. It looks so painted.
cosh0202	Peter M: Or to open a door you lock it.
cosh0203	Tim: Mm hm. Maybe that's just the formula for all the puzzles. You do something backwards.
cotg0101	Tim: One of the core things about adventure games we'd think about a lot was, what is keeping the player going forward? What is the reward? Because, you know, in RPG games you kill somebody, you get loot, you get items and stuff like that. Or you get rank, you know? So what is the reward for, um... playing adventure games? And it's environments, really.
cotg0102	Peter M: Yes, discovering a new place.
cotg0103	Tim: It's unlocking the new environment. I mean sometimes a cutscene and stuff, but really it's... environments and finding new places.
cotg0201	Peter M: The guy who played the, um, played these Peruvian flutes I believe is from Peru. Have to look at the credits again. He lives in the Mission. It's like the whole "Grim" score lives in the Mission.
cotg0301	Tim: And it's also spiritual in the way that it feels-- That's how you feel like, you feel like this is the end of the game. Because when you get to the part in the story where people are in the clouds, or in the heavens, and you start to hear the pan flute music you know you're kind of near the end of the story, somehow. But then you go back! And there's a whole other year, which creates a feeling of the game being super long. Like I'm playing "Mario" right now and there's a, like more and more levels-- After the end of the story there's more and more, which makes it feel really long, but if you just-- Like it ends at level five, and there's two more levels, but if you like had seven levels people wouldn't appreciate it. But if you end it at five, and then you have, "Oh oh, there's more levels!" It feels really long.
cotg0302	Peter M: It's like the, it's the encore effect.
cotg0303	Tim: Mm hm.
cobs0101	Dave: Alex, you had mentioned on our break that you were the master of playing through this game the most quickly.
cobs0102	Alex: That's right.
cobs0103	Dave: I think you should talk about that 'cause this-- When I played this game, I think it took me a couple of days.
cobs0104	Alex: Yeah. I think, I mean, I'm interested in playing the Remastered version to see how long it takes me. I think it'll be, you know, twelve hours or more. But when we were in test, obviously we were playing it every single day. We knew it inside and out. But when a brand new build came down we always had to play through from beginning to end, and I was the person that always ended up doing that 'cause I could do it the fastest. And I got down to just a little over an hour playthrough time. I wanna say like an hour and ten minutes was my fastest time. Yeah, it was um, it was something else, and I knew this--the whole game--like instinctually, and now I barely remember any of it.
cobs0105	Dave: You must have been able to skip through cutscenes or something--
cobs0106	Alex: Oh yeah, you can. And you can skip dialogue, too.
cobs0107	Dave: I think the cutscene tape that we received so we could watch them, I wanna say was almost two hours long.
cobs0108	Mark: It was like an hour and forty-five minutes, or something.
cobs0109	Paul: Good Lord.
cobs0110	Alex: I'd like to watch that. I mean, I guess we're watching it right now.
comt0101	Tim: I remember what you said once about playing I think it was the Day of the Dead theme... And you got the band to play that. And then you asked them like, "Hey, guys, would you, uh, is this something you might play? Like in your own band?" And they said, "Maybe on Halloween."
comt0102	Peter M: Yeah, that's right.
comt0103	Tim: Which I never-- It doesn't sound like a Halloween piece of music to me, but I guess they were just saying it was freaky.
comt0104	Peter M: Yeah.
comt0105	Tim: Or unusual.
comt0201	Tim: Now that's the ash tray that is-- The very first shot of the game and the very last shot of the game are the same, just like in "Sugarland Express." The shot of the little figurines around that ash tray is the same as the band that stands around this fountain later. That's how you prove that you took at least one film school class in college. You make the first shot the same as the last shot. Basic stuff.
cotd0101	Julian: It's funny 'cause Pete [McConnell] was always great at knowing-- Not only would he know it was gonna slip, he would always say, "Nah, nah..." And then he'd spew out a date. And he was always within weeks, you know. He always-- And sometimes that date was way past where we were being told the date was. In fact, it always was. But he was always right. And I don't know if it was just a sixth sense or if it was, um...
cotd0102	Clint: Well, I--
cotd0103	Julian: -- him putting it together.
cotd0104	Clint: I remember specifically what you're talking about. I'm not gonna be completely correct, but early, early in the year I predicted that it would be June, which was about two months past our deadline. And I felt very bold doing that.
cotd0105	Julian: Yeah, totally!
cotd0106	Clint: But I felt pretty-- On solid ground doing that. Of course this was internal, just within the sound group. We never would--
cotd0107	Julian: We never would say that to you.
cotd0108	Clint: -- share this with other people. And Pete immediately fired back: "Day of the Dead." We all said, "What?" "Day of the Dead." What is that, November fourth?
cotd0109	Lleslle: Um yeah, like fourth or fifth. November fifth.
cotd0110	Clint: And so, you know what, he was within-- We'd have to look at Wikipedia. Do you remember the release date? I don't--
cotd0111	Lleslle: Oh, it was October twenty... Oh man it's--
cotd0112	Julian: Close enough.
cotd0113	Lleslle: It was October.
cotd0114	Clint: Within a couple of weeks. He was within a couple of weeks and that was about March, that he did that.
cotd0115	Lleslle: Oh wow.
cotd0116	Clint: And it's quite amazing that he was able to see that distance. I don't know how he did it, but he said it with conviction, too, that's the funniest thing of all. And he ended up being right.
cotd0201	Tim: I love those cars. You don't even get to see them in the game much, but there's some neat concept art of them. They have pipes coming out the side of them, which is weird. It's like Glottis fixed them up. I think they're based on some actual snow cat combined with a--
cotd0202	Peter M: Oh yeah.
cotd0203	Tim: -- panel truck.
cotd0204	Tim: That's-- 'cause like we have, we'd have this list of things that are gonna be in the game, and nothing was just like, "A truck, make a truck." It was like, "No, let's get Peter Chan to design what a cool snow cat truck would look like in the thirties, but also kind of Mayan in a weird way."
cotd0205	Peter M: Yeah, it is kind of Mayan. That's funny.
cotd0206	Tim: Like everything will get designed.
coat0101	Peter M: There was a certain amount of improvisation that-- The more towards the end of the game you got, the more the musicians improvise. And so--
coat0102	Tim: Yeah, this sounds like the meadow, kind of, isn't it?
coat0103	Peter M: It is. It's a little bit like-- In fact, I think it's a remix of some of the tracks I used in the meadow. And they're sort of riffing on the Hector theme.
coat0104	Tim: Wait, how are they... Where is the Hector theme in this?
coat0105	Peter M: Um, the [sings music] is part of, um...
coat0106	Tim: [sings music] Oh my God! You are ripping this whole game wide open with this Hector conspiracy.
coat0107	Peter M: Kind of, yeah... What I do is hum the Hector theme to them before they would start jamming.
#creditscommentary	
cocr0101	Tim: Are we going to have commentary over the credits? This is where you, uh-- They do it on movies. This is where we see names and we're like, "Oh yeah, that guy was cool!"
cocr0102	Peter M: Yeah yeah yeah.
cocr0103	Tim: "Oh I forgot about her. She's cool." And then, "That Tim Schafer's a dirk--jerk." One of those guys is a jerk. I'm not gonna say who. Adam Schnitzer, I forgot to mention Adam Schnitzer. He did that room where you fill the coffins up with the materials. He was awesome. Sara Simon did textures along with Japeth. Jillian Moffett... C. Andrew Nelson, that's right. C. Andrew Nelson was one of the official Darth Vader appearance persons.
cocr0104	Peter M: Jeff did the mixing.
cocr0105	Tim: He was so tall that he could actually wear the costume.
cocr0106	Peter M: Hans Christian was the, uh, cello player.
cocr0107	Tim: There's a guy named Hans Christian?
cocr0108	Peter M: Hans Christian Reumschuessel, actually is his full name, but he just goes by Hans Christian.
cocr0109	Tim: Charango provided by Martin Schafer.
cocr0110	Peter M: Yeah.
cocr0201	Tim: Senior voice editor, Khris Brown, still hanging around! Causing trouble. Um... watching these credits, you get that weird feeling-- Like usually, you don't do stuff in sequence. You don't work from the beginning of the game to the end. You work on all parts, all around, usually. But this is the one thing where, like, these ending credits were the things we were doing... Like wiring this up happened the night before we shipped, or something, like we were really... And so we didn't-- We were like, "Can you approve the credits?" And we're just watching and we're like super tired, we gotta ship this game. Like, "Yeah, they look good. Yeah."
cocr0202	Tim: Race cat names by the "Grim Fandango" team. Oh, there were some race cat names that happened with the-- I sent out a message like, "Hey, can you guys help me think of--"
cocr0203	Peter M: Oh, that's right! The "Secrets of the Luftwaffe" theme. I'd forgotten Mike's theme was in there.
cocr0204	Tim: Wait, where is the--
cocr0205	Peter M: I think it's in the-- I think it might be in the blimp music?
cocr0206	Tim: That's an interesting--
cocr0207	Peter M: Yeah, I forgot all about that.
cocr0208	Tim: Me, I don't remember that even happening. You know I didn't play a lot of SWOTL. I played "Battle Britain," but not a lot of SWOTL.
cocr0301	Tim: ... and the box. But um, but yeah, so when you watch it I totally remember that weird last night feeling of... production. Which is always really weird, especially with the disc-based stuff. Before, the idea-- Now we think, "Ah, we'll fix it, we'll patch it," you know. I mean, we totally care about the quality of the game of course, but if something really, really bad happened, we'd patch it. But in a disc-based game, we weren't as into patching. It could happen but it-- So you really were like, "Oh God, this is-- I'm so nervous to ship this thing." Like saying it's done, saying you're not gonna fix any more bugs. It's done done was so much more final. So these last few days when you've just done everything you can for the game and you're just trying to wire the credits together in the last few minutes... So I'm feeling that stressful feeling right now.
cocr0302	Peter M: You know, it's the first ninety percent of a game is no big deal. It's the second ninety percent that's the tough...
cocr0303	Tim: Nice.
cocr0304	Peter M: That's the tough part. That's kind of--
cocr0305	Tim: Denise Gollaher! Still works at Double Fine. There you go.
cocr0401	Peter M: Steve Dauterman.
cocr0402	Tim: I'm not gonna mention any of those names. What? No, Steve Dauterman, he worked--
cocr0403	Peter M: He works in, uh--
cocr0404	Tim: He went to Australia but now he's somewhere else.
cocr0405	Peter M: Oh yeah. No, I just talked to him.
cocr0406	Tim: Alan Dundes... Alan Dundes was my anthropology teacher in Berkeley, where I learned about the Day of the Dead, that started this whole crazy thing. Spinray is, uh... you.
cocr0407	Peter M: Yeah, that's my band. What was that, what were you doing in there? Probably just special thanks.
cocr0408	Tim: These are special thanks, and the team got to pick--
cocr0409	Peter M: Yeah, 'cause they're kinda like my family. So when I wasn't at rehearsal, I think like-- Thanks for putting up with me not being there.
cocr0410	Tim: And I'm sure it meant a lot to them when they played this game. That's what I always say about the cats. One of my pet peeves, I hate cat and pet "thank yous" in credits.
cocr0411	Peter M: Oh yeah.
cocr0412	Tim: 'cause I'm like, okay, even if the cat figured out how to use Steam. Even if the cat figured out how to download this game--
cocr0413	Peter M: No that-- Good point.
cocr0414	Tim: How would they-- Would they even finish adventure-- How would they get past the sign post puzzle? Like that, there's no way a cat could get that far. They're not gonna see this-- Your "thank you."
cocr0501	Peter M: It's true. And probably they were just hanging out and enjoying themselves anyway.
cocr0502	Tim: Yeah. They have-- No way they'd have the patience for an adventure game. So with that thought, there we go. That's wrapping up the final... Thanks everybody for playing. Thanks for buying the remaster, or pirating it, however you got it. And thanks, Peter McConnell, for still working on games with me.
cocr0503	Peter M: Yeah, alright.
cocr0504	Tim: It's been a long time.
cocr0505	Peter M: Thank you.
cocr0506	Tim: I've learned a lot about "Grim Fandango" in this session, like how many times you got that one piece of music to work out for you. That was amazing. And it was really fun watching it again. And now I know how to solve all the puzzles for when I do my Let's Play of it, I'll actually act like I know what I'm doing. I don't wanna leave now 'cause I like the way my voice sounds.
cocr0507	Peter M: It's like basking in the glow, isn't it?
cocr0508	Tim: I wish everyone could hear me this clearly and loudly all the time. Alright! Thank you for playing. I'll say that now 'cause we never used to put that in the credits, and we do every time now, so we'll just say it now.
cocr0509	Peter M: Thanks for listening, too.
cocr0510	Tim: No one's gonna listen to this.
cocr0511	Peter M: Ah, that's right.

